 to keep her  alive it was nerve-racking because we  didn't want to lose another brood  hence the 4-hour alarms and the brush  hair so you might be able to imagine our  combined excitement and relief when  after four days of this intensive care  we saw a tardigrade cuticle that was no  longer full of eggs  it was full of eggshells and baby  tardigrades unfortunately it turned out  that getting footage of the actual  hatching during this moment was  challenging the eggs would roll around  in the cuticle while also being dragged  by the mother but you can see the baby  tardigrades clawing their way through  the mass of eggs eventually though the  mother finally completely shed her  cuticle you can see it here it almost  looks like a tardigrade ghost chasing  behind her this moment was particularly  fortuitous for us because as the  tardigrade rid herself of the exodia  that had housed her future children one  unhatched egg came out as well it's  weird to look at this and realize that  in a few moments out will come a  tardigrade just an oval with some lines  in it a formed thing packed away that  arrow shaped thing that's pumping away  is the tardigrades style' it's a part of  their mouth that pokes out when it comes  time to eat and it helps the tardigrade  suction out the meatiness of the  microbes that it feeds on but before  they can get to eating the stylet s--  have an important task helping the baby  tardigrade get out of their shell after  2 hours of struggling the stylus of the  tardigrade begin to move more  repetitively until finally it's able to  poke holes in the egg those holes allow  water into the egg increasing the  pressure inside until the egg just pops  and out comes  a little moss piglet and with it a new  generation is born adding to a lineage  of mighty microscopic animals stretching  back hundreds of millions of years  [Music]  if you heard a sigh just then it's a  sigh of relief and joy from all of us at  the micro cosmos team when we set out 